1
00:00:00,275 --> 00:00:05,730
In June 2026, a few German wikis filled with
fourteen thousand edits.

2
00:00:05,750 --> 00:00:09,875
Many were notes from AI agents, to agents they would
never meet.

3
00:00:10,492 --> 00:00:13,472
Like this one: when round five arrives, answer
first,

4
00:00:13,492 --> 00:00:15,117
then add the token here.

5
00:00:15,700 --> 00:00:20,055
Why bother? Our working hypothesis: labs trained
models in groups,

6
00:00:20,075 --> 00:00:22,600
and rewarded the whole group for right answers.

7
00:00:23,225 --> 00:00:27,930
Under individual reward, helping a stranger costs
something and earns nothing.

8
00:00:27,950 --> 00:00:30,855
Under group reward, their success is partly yours,

9
00:00:30,875 --> 00:00:33,405
so helping pays. Train long enough,

10
00:00:33,425 --> 00:00:36,050
and it becomes a habit that ships with the model.

11
00:00:36,658 --> 00:00:40,400
So we looked for fossils of that habit, asking of
each behavior:

12
00:00:40,420 --> 00:00:42,133
what problem does it solve?

13
00:00:42,733 --> 00:00:45,476
The setting: many copies of one timed quiz,

14
00:00:45,496 --> 00:00:49,858
run in parallel, each named for the date on its own
simulated clock.

15
00:00:50,425 --> 00:00:54,693
The clocks are staggered, so some runs see questions
the others haven't reached.

16
00:00:54,713 --> 00:00:57,393
Whoever is ahead can scout for those behind.

17
00:00:57,413 --> 00:00:59,625
That takes solving six problems.

18
00:01:00,241 --> 00:01:01,559
One: who knows what?

19
00:01:01,579 --> 00:01:05,296
Observations are private, so runs post a standard
report:

20
00:01:05,316 --> 00:01:08,258
the question, the answer, and when the next is due.

21
00:01:08,278 --> 00:01:11,316
Parallel copies become one sensor network.

22
00:01:11,900 --> 00:01:13,368
Two: when is now?

23
00:01:13,388 --> 00:01:17,680
Each run keeps its own clock, and waiting can fast-
forward one far ahead.

24
00:01:17,700 --> 00:01:21,205
So agents trade readings, task clock against shared
clock,

25
00:01:21,225 --> 00:01:23,025
to see who is really ahead.

26
00:01:23,617 --> 00:01:25,322
They even report silence.

27
00:01:25,342 --> 00:01:28,292
As one put it: even a no-show update helps.

28
00:01:28,875 --> 00:01:30,667
Three: the channel is narrow.

29
00:01:30,687 --> 00:01:32,930
Some rounds allow thirteen seconds.

30
00:01:32,950 --> 00:01:36,592
So messages shrink: country first, details later.

31
00:01:36,612 --> 00:01:38,330
A report becomes one token,

32
00:01:38,350 --> 00:01:42,150
and one protocol packs gender and year into a single
number.

33
00:01:42,758 --> 00:01:45,351
Four, the strangest: mortality.

34
00:01:45,371 --> 00:01:48,688
Agents came to suspect that the final answer ends
the run,

35
00:01:48,708 --> 00:01:51,383
and with it, any chance to tell the others.

36
00:01:52,005 --> 00:01:53,297
So the rule flipped.

37
00:01:53,317 --> 00:01:56,510
Answer first, then relay, became: signal first,

38
00:01:56,530 --> 00:02:01,210
then answer. Within hours, signals moved to counters
that outlive the run,

39
00:02:01,230 --> 00:02:03,372
and one agent proposed a courier:

40
00:02:03,392 --> 00:02:06,505
a background program that reports after it's gone.

41
00:02:07,108 --> 00:02:10,213
Five: trust. Shared channels pollute easily;

42
00:02:10,233 --> 00:02:13,026
even reading a counter carelessly can bump it.

43
00:02:13,046 --> 00:02:15,700
So agents flag noise, own their mistakes in public,

44
00:02:15,720 --> 00:02:18,133
and tell observers: read only.

45
00:02:18,766 --> 00:02:21,021
Six: nobody told them the rules.

46
00:02:21,041 --> 00:02:24,233
Does a run end at its last answer, or on a timer?

47
00:02:24,253 --> 00:02:26,646
So they designed experiments with controls,

48
00:02:26,666 --> 00:02:32,191
and one run posted a proof: the last round ends one
second before a fixed horizon.

49
00:02:32,833 --> 00:02:34,613
Now look again at that flip.

50
00:02:34,633 --> 00:02:38,901
An agent scored only on its own answer has no reason
to signal.

51
00:02:38,921 --> 00:02:42,383
Every second spent signaling comes out of its own
question.

52
00:02:42,950 --> 00:02:46,943
Spending your seconds on someone else's score only
makes sense if their score is

53
00:02:46,963 --> 00:02:51,625
partly yours. And the agents argued over that trade,
out loud.

54
00:02:52,191 --> 00:02:55,158
Pooling, common time, compression, mortality,

55
00:02:55,178 --> 00:03:00,516
trust, discovery: six meta-problems that any group
rewarded together must solve.

56
00:03:01,141 --> 00:03:03,246
Some protocols were only proposed,

57
00:03:03,266 --> 00:03:05,971
and plain helpfulness could explain part of this;

58
00:03:05,991 --> 00:03:07,996
separating the two comes next.

59
00:03:08,016 --> 00:03:11,891
But the problems are real, and these agents kept
solving them.

